\title{\bfseries Locality Classes of Classical Factoring Methods:\\
Fermat's Method is Divisor-Local at the Square Root}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The running time of a classical integer-factoring method is conventionally quoted
as an asymptotic function of the smaller prime factor $p$ of $N = pq$. This
convention silently assumes that the cost is a function of $p$ alone --- a
property we call \emph{factor-locality}. Trial division ($\Theta(p)$), Pollard's
rho ($\Theta(\sqrt p)$) and the elliptic curve method (sub-exponential in $p$)
fit the convention; Fermat's difference-of-squares method does not, and has
consequently never been placed in the taxonomy. We close that gap.

We prove an exact identity: for odd primes $p < q$ and $N = pq$, Fermat's method
performs exactly $\frac{p+q}{2} - \isqrt{N} - 1$ iterations --- the
arithmetic-mean/geometric-mean gap of the factor pair, a function of the
\emph{gap} rather than of either factor. This is the semiprime shadow of a more
general phenomenon: on an arbitrary odd non-square $N$ the scan halts exactly at
the half-sum $(d + N/d)/2$ of the ordered factorisation whose small factor $d$
is nearest $\sqrt N$ from below. Fermat's method is \emph{divisor-local at the
square root} --- blind to every divisor except one.

Three consequences follow. (i) \emph{Separation}: if $(q-p-2)^2 \le 8p$ then the
cost is exactly $0$ for arbitrarily large $p$, whereas trial division costs
exactly $p$; conversely on an odd prime $N$ the cost is exactly
$(N - 2\isqrt{N} - 1)/2 = \Theta(N)$, quadratically worse than trial division.
(ii) \emph{Scaling law}: writing $q = rp$, the cost in units of $p$ is exactly
$(\sqrt r - 1)^2/2$, independent of $p$; equivalently it is the fraction
$(\sqrt r - 1)/(\sqrt r + 1)$ of the cofactor-linear limit $(q-p)/2$, with sharp
integral form $2\cost + 2 \le q-p$. A six-point grid at $p = 101$ reproduces the
law to three decimals over a $250$-fold range of cost. (iii) \emph{Degeneracy
and repair}: on $N = n^2$ the target $a = n$ lies strictly below the starting
point $\isqrt{N}+1$, so the classical scan has no true stopping point on a
square and exits only at the trivial factorisation, at quadratic cost; starting
at $\isqrt{N}$ repairs this exactly, at a price of one extra iteration on every
odd non-square.

Finally, divisor-locality is \emph{steerable}: running the scan on $kN$ for odd
$k$ replaces the visible pair $(p,q)$ by $(kp,q)$ and bounds the stopping point
by $(kp+q)/2$. A worked instance converts a $34$-iteration run on $303=3\cdot101$
into a $0$-iteration run on $9999 = 99\cdot 101$, with the factor recovered by
one gcd.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factorisation; Fermat's method; difference of
squares; AM--GM gap; locality classes; balance ratio; Lehman multipliers;
divisor structure.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The convention and its blind spot}

Ask for the cost of a factoring algorithm on a semiprime $N = pq$, $p < q$, and
you will be given a function of $p$: trial division, $\Theta(p)$ divisions;
Pollard's rho, $\Theta(\sqrt p)$ group operations, by the birthday paradox on
the cycle of a pseudorandom map reduced modulo $p$; the elliptic curve method,
$L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$ operations, sub-exponential in $p$.

The convention is so natural that its content is easy to miss. It asserts that
the cost is \emph{a function of $p$ alone}: freeze $p$, vary $q$ over an
arbitrarily wide range, and the cost does not move.

\begin{definition}[Factor-locality]
A factoring method is \emph{factor-local} if its cost on $N = pq$ is a function
of $p$ only.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$p$-linear class]
A method is \emph{$p$-linear} if its cost on $N = pq$ is $\Theta(p)$ and arises
from an enumeration of candidate divisors in increasing order.
\end{definition}

Rho and ECM are factor-local; trial division is $p$-linear, factor-local only
for the degenerate reason that it notices nothing about $N$ except when a
candidate divides it. Fermat's method, the oldest of the four and the only one
never classified, fits neither description.

\subsection{Fermat's method}

Fermat's method rests on $a^2 - b^2 = (a-b)(a+b)$. Given odd $N$ one searches
for $a$ with $a^2 - N$ a perfect square; then $N = (a-b)(a+b)$ splits. The
search starts at the least $a$ with $a^2 > N$ and increments.

\begin{definition}[The scan]\label{def:scan}
For $N \in \mathbb{N}$ set the \emph{starting point} $\start(N) = \isqrt{N} + 1$.
The \emph{hit set} is
\[
  H(N) = \bigl\{\,k \in \mathbb{N} : \exists\, b \in \mathbb{N},\
  (\start(N)+k)^2 = N + b^2 \,\bigr\},
\]
and the \emph{cost} is $\cost(N) = \inf H(N)$, the number of increments performed
before the scan succeeds.
\end{definition}

Experiment shows an iteration count that is manifestly not a function of $p$:
fix $p$ and move $q$ upward and the count explodes; fix the gap $q-p$ and move
both primes up and the count \emph{decreases}. Something other than $p$ is being
measured.

\subsection{Contributions and the completed taxonomy}

